WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.799
Level 6 priced one way to be wrong.

2
00:00:02.899 --> 00:00:05.948
Here is the other one, in plain sight.

3
00:00:06.033 --> 00:00:10.407
The limit is not a verdict handed down. It is a distance,

4
00:00:10.507 --> 00:00:14.645
stepped out from the centre one sigma width at a time.

5
00:00:14.733 --> 00:00:18.435
Stop at the third one and draw the line. That line

6
00:00:18.535 --> 00:00:21.857
does not move again for the rest of this act.

7
00:00:21.950 --> 00:00:25.738
Before I move anything: decide where you would have to

8
00:00:25.838 --> 00:00:30.130
put the mean before this chart starts complaining. Hold that.

9
00:00:30.716 --> 00:00:34.967
Take a copy of that same curve and move the process by

10
00:00:35.067 --> 00:00:39.640
1 sigma. Same shape, same spread. Only the mean has moved.

11
00:00:39.733 --> 00:00:44.050
The process has genuinely moved, and the picture of it looks

12
00:00:44.150 --> 00:00:48.321
almost exactly like the picture of a process that has not.

13
00:00:48.416 --> 00:00:53.180
Both of these errors start at the same line, so let the line cut

14
00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:58.804
them out. This tail is the process behaving and the chart shouting anyway.

15
00:00:58.900 --> 00:01:01.964
And this one is the process having moved,

16
00:01:02.064 --> 00:01:05.592
with every point still landing inside the line.

17
00:01:05.683 --> 00:01:09.053
One of these is the error the limit was chosen

18
00:01:09.153 --> 00:01:12.447
against. The other is the one you are paying.

19
00:01:12.533 --> 00:01:15.079
Now they can be named. This one is

20
00:01:15.179 --> 00:01:18.193
crying wolf. That one is staying silent.

21
00:01:18.283 --> 00:01:22.671
And a name that has been earned can shrink to one letter.

22
00:01:22.766 --> 00:01:26.539
The sliver is the rate the limit was designed to

23
00:01:26.639 --> 00:01:30.250
hold down. The bulk is the rate nobody quotes.

24
00:01:30.350 --> 00:01:33.898
So put a handle on the mean and let it walk.

25
00:01:33.983 --> 00:01:38.071
Point at the shift where you think this chart catches half of

26
00:01:38.171 --> 00:01:41.779
what it is shown. The handle is about to walk past it.

27
00:01:42.816 --> 00:01:47.759
Small shifts do almost nothing. The blue bulk barely moves and the

28
00:01:47.859 --> 00:01:52.190
column barely lifts, because one point at a time is a weak

29
00:01:52.290 --> 00:01:56.468
test and a small move looks exactly like ordinary noise.

30
00:01:56.566 --> 00:02:02.298
Then it wakes up. Nothing about the chart changed. The mean simply got close enough

31
00:02:02.398 --> 00:02:06.162
to the line for the tail to be on the wrong side of it.

32
00:02:06.250 --> 00:02:10.561
The mean is sitting on the line. Half the curve is each side

33
00:02:10.661 --> 00:02:14.238
of it, so the column is at half, and it had to be.

34
00:02:15.433 --> 00:02:19.532
There is an obvious escape route here: stop moving the

35
00:02:19.632 --> 00:02:23.109
process and move the line instead. Pull it in.

36
00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:28.530
The column does rise earlier. It also just fattened the red sliver, and

37
00:02:28.630 --> 00:02:33.426
that sliver is the alarms you will chase on a process that never

38
00:02:33.526 --> 00:02:38.628
moved. Sensitivity bought this way is paid for in the same currency.

39
00:02:38.716 --> 00:02:42.264
So put the line back where Level 6 left it.

40
00:02:42.350 --> 00:02:47.296
Which is all the power expression says. One term for the tail on the

41
00:02:47.396 --> 00:02:51.898
far side, one for the tail on the near side, and nothing else.

42
00:02:51.983 --> 00:02:56.052
One point, one chance. That is the whole limitation,

43
00:02:56.152 --> 00:02:59.179
and this is its price in points waited.

44
00:02:59.266 --> 00:03:02.286
Keep the board. Drop the instruments.

45
00:03:02.383 --> 00:03:07.203
One subgroup. It lands here, comfortably inside the line.

46
00:03:07.300 --> 00:03:09.792
One word. In, or out.

47
00:03:10.333 --> 00:03:15.093
In. The rule the chart owns is a distance, the point is inside

48
00:03:15.193 --> 00:03:19.953
the distance, and that is the whole answer it is able to give.

49
00:03:20.050 --> 00:03:23.756
But the same point carries something the verdict threw

50
00:03:23.856 --> 00:03:27.210
away: how much of the in-control curve is further

51
00:03:27.310 --> 00:03:30.030
out than it is. Peel that off the board.

52
00:03:30.116 --> 00:03:34.678
Two objects now, built from one point, and they disagree. The

53
00:03:34.778 --> 00:03:38.728
chart's answer is a pass. The evidence is not a pass.

54
00:03:38.816 --> 00:03:42.120
Walk the point out to the line itself, and the

55
00:03:42.220 --> 00:03:46.116
two answers agree. The peeled tail lands on the sliver

56
00:03:46.216 --> 00:03:49.372
from earlier and the chip lands on its rate.

57
00:03:49.466 --> 00:03:53.926
So the agreement holds at exactly one place. Step back inside,

58
00:03:54.026 --> 00:03:58.191
and the disagreement is a property of the rule rather than

59
00:03:58.291 --> 00:04:02.309
of the drawing. The verdict is not the evidence, and the

60
00:04:02.409 --> 00:04:05.398
chart keeps one and throws the other away.

61
00:04:05.483 --> 00:04:08.671
Hold on to all of that and make room.

62
00:04:08.766 --> 00:04:11.751
The board goes up there and stays there.

63
00:04:11.851 --> 00:04:14.450
Everything on it is the same board.

64
00:04:14.550 --> 00:04:17.258
Because a rule is a pattern in a run

65
00:04:17.358 --> 00:04:21.002
of subgroups, and a run needs somewhere to fall.

66
00:04:21.100 --> 00:04:25.584
This is the whole purchase, on one axis of subgroups. How

67
00:04:25.684 --> 00:04:30.248
long between false alarms, and how long until a real shift

68
00:04:30.348 --> 00:04:34.752
is caught. Both are simulated run lengths, not opinions.

69
00:04:34.850 --> 00:04:39.483
Both bars are frozen at their single-rule lengths. Before I

70
00:04:39.583 --> 00:04:43.414
switch anything on: which way does each one move?

71
00:04:43.950 --> 00:04:47.881
Stop one is the rule Level 6 already bought: a single

72
00:04:47.981 --> 00:04:52.370
point outside the line. Those are the lengths it gives you.

73
00:04:52.466 --> 00:04:57.085
Stop two adds a pattern that no single point could hold — two

74
00:04:57.185 --> 00:05:01.726
of three out at two sigma on the same side. Watch both bars.

75
00:05:01.816 --> 00:05:06.876
Stop three switches the last two on, and the bars do it again.

76
00:05:08.816 --> 00:05:16.689
That all-four false-alarm length was published in 1987 as 91.75. This simulation

77
00:05:16.789 --> 00:05:21.772
was never told about it, and it landed on the tick.

78
00:05:21.866 --> 00:05:25.880
So measure the two moves. This is what the purchase spent,

79
00:05:25.980 --> 00:05:29.710
and this is what it bought. Lay one against the other.

80
00:05:29.800 --> 00:05:33.742
Every rule you switch on buys time and spends alarms.

81
00:05:33.842 --> 00:05:38.090
Both sides are simulated, not asserted, and at this shift

82
00:05:38.190 --> 00:05:41.676
the two lengths are near enough to argue about.

83
00:05:41.766 --> 00:05:47.410
Nothing about the board changes now. One thing changes: the shift this

84
00:05:47.510 --> 00:05:51.266
line is trying to catch goes from 1 sigma to 3.

85
00:05:51.366 --> 00:05:54.690
The false-alarm bar is not going to be touched,

86
00:05:54.790 --> 00:05:58.186
because nothing about a false alarm depends on a

87
00:05:58.286 --> 00:06:01.610
shift that did not happen. Watch it hold still.

88
00:06:01.700 --> 00:06:04.617
Now walk the same three stops again.

89
00:06:04.717 --> 00:06:07.552
Same rules, same order, same price.

90
00:06:07.650 --> 00:06:11.987
There is the same measurement. Rule one already sees a jump

91
00:06:12.087 --> 00:06:15.823
that big on the very next point, so the extra rules

92
00:06:15.923 --> 00:06:20.486
were bought at exactly the previous price and then never used.

93
00:06:20.583 --> 00:06:24.804
Same purchase. The cost did not move. The benefit nearly

94
00:06:24.904 --> 00:06:28.585
vanished. The rules did not get worse — the shift

95
00:06:28.685 --> 00:06:32.675
you are aiming at changed, and that alone decided it.

96
00:06:32.766 --> 00:06:36.362
Which leaves exactly one question standing.

